*Drawing to be made by the candidate: a large side (lateral) view of an Agama lizard, drawn with sharp continuous lines, in proportion, with a title ("Lateral view of an Agama lizard") and straight label lines that do not cross.*

**Parts to draw and label:**

- **Head**: triangular and flattened, bearing a **mouth** with a tongue, a pair of **nostrils** at the tip of the snout, a pair of lateral **eyes** protected by **upper and lower eyelids**, and, behind each eye, a **tympanic membrane (ear drum)** visible at the surface (there is no external ear flap);
- **Neck**: short and flexible, joining the head to the trunk, allowing the head to nod;
- **Trunk**: the body, covered with dry **scales** (horny, overlapping, waterproof skin);
- **Four limbs** (two **forelimbs** and two **hindlimbs**), each with **five clawed digits** (toes) used for gripping and climbing;
- **Tail**: long and tapering, used for balance;
- **Cloaca/vent** on the underside at the base of the tail;
- **Skin**: dry, scaly, brightly coloured on the head and neck in the male (orange-red head, blue-grey body), dull brown in the female.

**If described instead:** the answer must name the head, eyes with eyelids, nostrils, tympanum, neck, trunk, forelimbs, hindlimbs with clawed digits, tail and the scaly skin, and say what each does.
